A Coupled Contact–Structure Framework for Assessing Annular Compliance in Multi-Row Ball Bearings: 

Derivation, Verification Protocol, and Planned Falsification of the Distributed Load Management Ring

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This thesis develops a coupled contact–structure–life prediction framework for the Distributed Load Management Ring (DLMR), a thin compliant outer ring with local flexures interposed between the raceway and the wheel crown in multi-row ball bearings. The work is structured as a falsifiable research programme: it specifies the mathematical foundations, the verification protocol for each computational module, and a decision framework capable of rejecting the architecture if its benefits do not exceed combined modelling and numerical uncertainty.

The framework integrates: (i) an elliptical Hertzian contact formulation with explicit validity checks and a rigorously bounded ellipticity domain; (ii) a thin-ring structural model whose Green's function is presented as a working hypothesis pending independent derivation and full-field finite-element validation; (iii) a coupled fixed-point iteration between contact solver and structural model, with a declared convergence criterion that must be verified numerically rather than asserted; (iv) an energy balance expressed as an explicit accounting identity over all declared dissipative channels, including rolling, sliding, seal, drag, and flexure hysteresis; and (v) life prediction according to the full multi-row Formula 14 of ISO 16281:2025 with the ball-bearing Weibull modulus e = 10/9.

The thesis does not claim demonstrated numerical performance. A preliminary audit established that the initial circular-contact benchmark, the dimensional consistency of the ring Green's function, and the ISO combination rule required correction before any DLMR conclusion could be drawn. The present version incorporates those corrections: the Hertzian circular benchmark is restated with the correct semi-axis and maximum pressure; the ring derivation is reformulated as a hypothesis to be proven; the ISO implementation is described in terms of the normative per-row, per-raceway assembly; and the flexure stiffness is computed for the correct fixed-guided boundary condition. The numerical outcomes previously presented as validated are reclassified as illustrative targets that must be reproduced by independent verification before they can support any claim.

The thesis makes three contributions: a reusable, falsifiable framework for annular-compliance bearing architectures; a verification protocol with explicit pass criteria for four independent modules; and a decision framework that treats a negative result as scientifically informative if it identifies the physical constraints that prevent the architecture from achieving a net benefit.

Keywords: compliant bearing, elliptical Hertzian contact, circular ring Green's function, load distribution, ISO 16281:2025, energy dissipation, falsification methodology

