Introduction

Context and Motivation

Multi-row ball bearings in automotive wheel hubs operate under combined radial, axial, and moment loads. The load distribution among rolling elements is inherently non-uniform: the position of the loaded zone, the stiffness of the surrounding structure, and the geometric conformity of the raceways all determine which contacts carry the majority of the external load. This non-uniformity accelerates fatigue at the most heavily loaded contacts and influences frictional dissipation.

A conventional response is to increase bearing size or preload, both of which carry mass and friction penalties. An alternative is to introduce controlled compliance in the outer ring, allowing the raceway to deform in a manner that redistributes load among the balls. The general principle of load redistribution through annular compliance is the subject of existing prior art, including EP3163100A1, which discloses a distributed-load bearing architecture with an inner flex ring. The present thesis does not claim novelty of this general principle. It asks a narrower and more defensible question: under what conditions, if any, can a deliberately compliant annular support produce a net benefit that survives explicit falsification against a controlled rigid-ring comparator, without degrading raceway geometry, structural stiffness, or corrected fatigue life?

The DLMR Concept

The Distributed Load Management Ring (DLMR) is a thin outer ring (mean radius R_c \approx 45 mm, radial thickness h_c \approx 2.5 mm) connected to the wheel crown through local flexures (length L_f \approx 8 mm, width b_f \approx 1.2 mm, thickness t_f \approx 0.6 mm). The flexures provide a designed radial stiffness k_f that is neither perfectly rigid nor freely compliant. The raceway is machined into the inner surface of this ring. Under external load, the ring deforms elastically, and the raceway geometry—normally treated as a rigid boundary condition—becomes an output of the structural model.

Revised Research Objective

This research investigates whether a deliberately compliant annular support can redistribute internal contact loads in multi-row angular-contact ball bearings while preserving raceway geometry, structural stiffness, fatigue-life performance, and acceptable energy dissipation.

The study develops and independently verifies a coupled contact–structure framework before applying it to the DLMR architecture. The framework is designed to distinguish genuine load-redistribution effects from numerical artefacts, geometric changes that invalidate Hertzian assumptions, and apparent friction reductions that are offset by additional structural dissipation.

No performance advantage is assumed in advance. The DLMR hypothesis is considered supported only if the predicted benefits remain robust across a declared operating domain, exceed the combined modelling and numerical uncertainty, and are not obtained at the expense of bearing stiffness, raceway integrity, or fatigue-life requirements. A negative result is considered scientifically informative if the validated framework identifies the physical or structural constraints that prevent the architecture from achieving a net benefit.

Research Hypotheses

The thesis tests five hypotheses:

H1 — Load equalisation. The DLMR reduces contact-force concentration relative to a rigid-ring comparator (Comparator A) over a declared set of load cases, using a primary indicator specified in advance.

H2 — Energy benefit. The DLMR produces a net reduction in total bearing dissipated power over a representative cycle, after accounting for all declared dissipative channels, and the gain exceeds the combined uncertainty U_{\text{total}}.

H3 — Hertzian validity. The raceway geometry remains compatible with elliptical Hertzian contact theory for all active contacts retained in the life calculation, i.e., the semi-axes remain strictly inferior to the available geometric margins.

H4 — Life maintenance or improvement. The modified reference rating life L_{10r} computed according to ISO 16281:2025 is greater than or equal to that of Comparator A.

H5 — Robustness. The benefits claimed under H1 and H2 persist under declared perturbations: degraded lubricant conditions, realistic geometric defects, load transients, and progressive stiffness degradation.

Methodological Status of This Version

This version of the thesis is a research programme and verification protocol, not a report of demonstrated results. The programme is organised in four independently verified modules (M1–M4) followed by a coupled DLMR analysis (M5) and a robustness campaign (M6). Chapters 3 and 4 describe the implementation and the planned falsification campaign; they do not present validated numerical outcomes.

This distinction is deliberate. A preliminary audit of an earlier draft identified three classes of deficiency that had to be corrected before any DLMR conclusion could be drawn: a numerical error in the circular-contact benchmark, a dimensional inconsistency in the ring Green's function derivation, and a non-normative simplification of the multi-row ISO life combination. These corrections are incorporated below, and the affected results are reclassified accordingly.

Declared Limits

The thesis does not claim novelty of the principle of a flexible ring equalising loads. Prior art exists, and any remaining novelty must be established through a claim-by-claim patent review and a technical comparison of the proposed wheel-hub architecture with the disclosed embodiments.

Manufacturing defects are outside the base model and are treated as parametric perturbations in the robustness phase. Experimental validation is not presented; no conclusion on real superiority can be drawn before bench comparison. No life equation is presented as a certified reproduction of the standard unless the full normative text has been consulted and reference cases reproduced. The scope of the energy balance is the complete bearing over a representative cycle; a gain on bearing power is not automatically a gain on vehicle energy.

