Chapter 1 — Literature Review and Theoretical Positioning

1.1 Load Distribution in Rolling Bearings

The classical treatment of load distribution assumes rigid raceways and computes contact forces from geometric interference under external load. For a single-row bearing under radial load, the load distribution is given by the Stribeck relation; for multi-row bearings under combined load, it requires simultaneous solution of equilibrium equations for each row and each angular position.

The rigid-raceway assumption is convenient but not universally valid. The literature distinguishes three sources of compliance that modify load distribution: elastic deformation of the bearing ring itself, deformation of the surrounding housing, and local raceway compliance in the vicinity of individual contacts. The DLMR architecture introduces a fourth category: designed annular compliance through local flexures whose stiffness is a design parameter, distinct from passive ring flexibility.

The qualitative claim that flexible rings reduce maximum contact force is supported by prior work on large slewing bearings. This prior work provides an external order-of-magnitude indication that annular compliance can be beneficial; it does not provide a quantitative target for an automotive hub bearing, because the geometry, scale, and loading conditions differ substantially.

1.2 Elliptical Hertzian Contact

The Hertzian theory of elliptical contact remains the foundation for bearing contact analysis. For a ball–raceway contact, the principal curvatures in the rolling and transverse directions determine the contact ellipse semi-axes a and b, the maximum pressure p_0, and the normal approach \delta. The ellipticity parameter relates to the curvature difference through complete elliptic integrals.

The formulation used here is an elliptical Hertzian contact formulation with explicit validity checks. The theory is not "geometrically exact": it relies on a local parabolic approximation of the contacting surfaces and on specific elastic assumptions (small strain, homogeneous isotropic material, frictionless normal contact, half-space approximation). The validity checks are therefore not incidental; they are part of the formulation.

Two points require particular care. First, the mapping between the curvature difference and the ellipticity parameter must be treated as a well-posed equation over a rigorously stated domain, not as a formula whose limiting behaviour is taken for granted. Second, the sign convention for the principal curvatures must be constructed explicitly from the actual surfaces and their common normal, separately for the inner raceway, the outer raceway, and each row. It cannot be assigned once and reused across geometries.

1.3 Thin Circular Ring Mechanics

The radial deflection of a thin circular ring under concentrated or distributed loads is a classical problem. Closed-form solutions exist for specific load cases; modal or Fourier methods provide the full displacement field.

The modal approach is more general but requires careful treatment of the load definition, the Fourier normalisation, and the rigid-body modes. A point force is not equivalent to a distributed load per unit angle; the Fourier coefficients differ by a factor of 2\pi. The prefactor of the modal sum must arise from the differential equation and the load definition, not from an ad hoc correction introduced after the fact. The elastic modes do not constrain the two rigid-body modes; a flexibility matrix built from elastic modes alone is singular and must be regularised by an independent stiffness contribution before inversion.

In the present thesis, the ring Green's function is presented as a working hypothesis and is not used in the coupled model until it has been independently derived with dimensionally consistent load definitions and validated against a three-dimensional finite-element model under sectorial loading. This explicit status is a deliberate methodological choice: an unproven Green's function would contaminate every downstream conclusion about load distribution, fatigue, and energy.

1.4 Life Prediction Standards

ISO 16281:2025 defines the calculation of the modified reference rating life for rolling bearings, accounting for lubrication, contamination, radial clearance, and tilt. The standard distinguishes the basic rating life from the modified reference rating life and, for multi-row bearings, specifies a per-row and per-raceway assembly. The Weibull modulus for ball bearings is e = 10/9; the load–life exponent is p = 3 for point contacts and p = 10/3 for line contacts.

The thesis distinguishes rigorously between the basic rating life, the modified reference rating life, the per-row and per-raceway contributions, and the damage accumulation over a usage spectrum. A simple Weibull combination of row lives is retained only as a conceptual illustration and is explicitly disclaimed as a non-normative simplification. The full multi-row formula is implemented in module M4.

1.5 Energy Dissipation in Bearings

Bearing friction is the sum of several mechanisms: rolling friction due to elastic hysteresis, sliding friction in the contact ellipse and at the cage, seal friction, and drag loss due to lubricant churning. A model that captures only sliding power omits three other dissipative channels and cannot be presented as a complete bearing energy balance.

The thesis computes the sliding dissipation by surface integration over the contact area with a declared traction model, and includes the rolling, seal, and drag moments as declared contributions to the total. The sliding power is defined as a positive-definite dissipation with an explicit traction and relative-velocity convention. Flexure hysteresis is computed from the stress–strain cycle in the flexure volume, with the cyclic frequency determined from the load history rather than from the wheel rotational frequency.

