2.2 Thin-Ring Structural Model: A Working Hypothesis

2.2.1 Status of the Green's Function

The radial deflection of a thin circular ring under a point load is a classical problem whose treatment in the literature requires careful normalisation. In this thesis, the modal Green's function is presented as a working hypothesis, not as a demonstrated result. It is withheld from the coupled implementation until two conditions are met: (i) a dimensionally consistent derivation from the curved-beam equilibrium equation, with an explicit load definition; and (ii) validation against a three-dimensional finite-element model under sectorial loading, using a full-field comparison rather than a single scalar.

An earlier draft presented a derivation that was dimensionally inconsistent: the term R_c^4 q / EI has units of area, while the left-hand side has units of length. That derivation is withdrawn. The correct approach is to begin from the curved-beam equilibrium equation with a load density defined in N/rad or N/m, derive the modal response with consistent units, and only then construct the Green's function. The 1/\pi prefactor must arise from the Fourier normalisation of the load, not be introduced as a correction after the fact.

2.2.2 Singularity of the Flexibility Matrix and Regularisation

Independently of the numerical values of the Green's function, the flexibility matrix built from elastic modes n \geq 2 is singular: it does not constrain the rigid-body modes n = 0 (uniform radial translation) and n = 1 (differential translation). The naive inversion

\mathbf{K}_c = \mathbf{F}_{\text{ring}}^{-1} + \operatorname{diag}(k_f)

is mathematically ill-posed. The correct formulation treats the ring as a curved beam on distributed elastic foundations:

\mathbf{K}_{\text{global}} = \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} + \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}}

where \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} is the stiffness matrix assembled from elastic modes and \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} is the stiffness matrix of the flexures on the same generalised coordinates.

Proposition 2 (Regularisation by flexures). If the flexure stiffness matrix \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} is positive definite on the subspace of rigid-body modes \{n = 0, 1\}, then \mathbf{K}_{\text{global}} is positive definite and invertible.

Proof. \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} is positive semi-definite with null space spanned by the rigid-body modes. \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} is positive definite on the same subspace by hypothesis. Their sum is positive definite. \square

This proposition is correct in its algebraic statement. It does not establish that the ring flexibility matrix is itself correct, nor that the real flexures provide the positive-definite support required. Both must be verified independently.

2.2.3 Flexure Stiffness: Analytical Model and Required Verification

The DLMR flexures are designed as fixed-guided beams (both ends fixed against rotation, one end displaced transversely). For that boundary condition, the radial stiffness is:

k_f = \frac{12 EI_f}{L_f^3}, \qquad I_f = \frac{b_f t_f^3}{12}

For the nominal dimensions and steel (E = 210 GPa, b_f = 1.2 mm, t_f = 0.6 mm, L_f = 8 mm), the moment of inertia is I_f = 2.16 \times 10^{-14} m^4, and the analytical stiffness is:

k_f \approx 1.06 \times 10^5 \text{ N/m}

This value is the analytical ideal for a prismatic fixed-guided beam. It assumes uniform geometry, idealised boundary conditions, linear deformation, no interaction with adjacent sectors, and a response dominated by bending. The effective stiffness of the integrated flexure must be extracted from the three-dimensional model with the actual assembly conditions and compared against the analytical value. The analytical value is not used in the coupled model until that comparison has been made.

