2.3 Coupled Contact–Structure Formulation

2.3.1 Kinematic Chain and Sign Convention

The mechanical chain is defined explicitly as: outer raceway \leftrightarrow DLMR ring \leftrightarrow flexures \leftrightarrow crown \leftrightarrow knuckle. Each interface is assigned degrees of freedom, compatibility conditions, and a constitutive law. The DLMR is treated as a distinct part; the connection between raceway and ring is stated (bonded or contact), and the crown is treated as a rigid body with three translational and three rotational degrees of freedom in the current phase.

The decomposition

\mathbf{u}_{\text{raceway}} = \mathbf{u}_{\text{support}} + \mathbf{u}_{\text{ring}} + \mathbf{u}_{\text{local}}

is retained as an organisational device. It is not a demonstrated superposition. If the finite-element model of the ring already includes the local raceway deformation, then \mathbf{u}_{\text{local}} must not be added a second time. The implementation must document, for each deformation component, which model produces it, so that double-counting is prevented by construction.

The normal compression of contact ij is defined from geometric distances between the deformed surfaces:

\delta_{ij} = \delta_{0,ij} + \mathbf{n}_{ij}^{\mathsf T} \mathbf{B}_{ij} \mathbf{q} - \mathbf{g}_{ij}^{\mathsf T} \mathbf{x}_c - \Delta g_{ij}(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ring}})

with the sign convention that an increase in \delta_{ij} always corresponds to an increase in normal compression and in contact force. The term \Delta g_{ij}(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ring}}) is computed by the structural model and fed back into the contact solver at each iteration. Whether it increases or decreases the gap is determined by the geometry, not by convention.

2.3.2 Contact Problem Completeness

The kinematic equation for \delta_{ij} is not, by itself, a contact law. The coupled contact problem must satisfy, for every contact:

Q_i \geq 0, \qquad g_i \geq 0, \qquad Q_i g_i = 0

where g_i is the residual gap, with a convention consistent with \delta_{ij}. For an active contact, the Hertzian relation links Q_i to \delta_{ij} through the local geometry and elastic properties. The equilibrium of each row and of the crown must be satisfied simultaneously, together with the row-to-row load sharing under the applied external loads, preload, and clearance.

An earlier draft presented only the kinematic equation and treated it as sufficient. The present version states explicitly that a complete contact formulation includes the unilateral conditions, the normal law, the equilibrium equations for both rows, the degrees of freedom of balls and crown, the preload and clearance conditions, and the distribution of external loads among the rows.

2.3.3 Iterative Solution and Convergence

The coupled system is solved by a fixed-point iteration:

1. Initialise \mathbf{u}_{\text{ring}}^{(0)} = \mathbf{0}.
2. Solve the contact problem with raceway geometry \mathbf{g} + \Delta g(\mathbf{u}_{\text{ring}}^{(k)}) to obtain Q_i^{(k)}.
3. Apply Q_i^{(k)} to the structural model to obtain \mathbf{u}_{\text{ring}}^{(k+1)}.
4. Repeat until the relative update falls below \varepsilon_{\text{tol}} = 10^{-6}.

The contraction condition

\|\mathbf{K}_{\text{struct}}^{-1} \mathbf{J}_{\text{contact}}\| < 1

is stated as a sufficient condition for a particular iteration, not as a general proof of convergence of the nonlinear system. The norms, the function spaces, and the variables on which the Jacobian acts must be specified before the condition is used. Convergence must be verified numerically under multiple initialisations, multiple load levels, and with relaxation if the plain fixed-point iteration stalls. A monolithic Newton method with line search is retained as a comparison and fallback.

