2.4 Energy Balance: A Complete Accounting Identity

2.4.1 Positive-Definite Sliding Dissipation

The sliding dissipation is defined as a positive-definite quantity with an explicit traction and velocity convention:

P_{sl,\text{diss}} = -\sum_c \int_{A_c} \boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} \cdot \mathbf{v}_{sl,c} \, dA

where \boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} is the tangential traction on body 1 and \mathbf{v}_{sl,c} is the relative sliding velocity, with the sign convention stated in the implementation documentation. The tangential traction model is declared as a simplification in phase N1; validation by a full EHL model is reserved for phase N2.

2.4.2 Complete Bearing Power Accounting

The total bearing power is expressed as an explicit sum over all declared dissipative channels:

P_{\text{total}} = P_{rr} + P_{sl} + P_{\text{seal}} + P_{\text{drag}} + P_{\text{flex}}

where P_{rr} is rolling friction due to elastic hysteresis, P_{sl} is sliding dissipation, P_{\text{seal}} is seal friction, P_{\text{drag}} is drag loss due to lubricant churning, and P_{\text{flex}} is flexure hysteresis.

The net gain is defined as:

P_{\text{net}} = P_{\text{total,ref}} - P_{\text{total,DLMR}}

A reduction on one channel (for example, sliding) is not, by itself, a net gain. The published result must report each channel separately, its fraction of the total in the reference configuration, and the uncertainty of each estimate. An earlier draft reported a 6.2% reduction in sliding dissipation and a 1.8% flexure dissipation, and concluded to a 4.1% net gain without publishing the accounting equation. That conclusion is withdrawn pending the complete accounting.

2.4.3 Flexure Hysteresis

The power dissipated by hysteresis in the flexures is:

P_{\text{flex}} = f_{\text{flex}} \int_{V_{\text{flex}}} \left[ \oint_{\text{cycle}} \boldsymbol{\sigma} : d\boldsymbol{\varepsilon} \right] dV

where f_{\text{flex}} is the actual frequency of cyclic loading of the flexures, determined from the load history, not from the wheel rotational frequency.

