2.5 Life Prediction: ISO 16281:2025 with Full Multi-Row Assembly

2.5.1 Notation

The thesis distinguishes rigorously:

· L_{10}: basic rating life.
· L_{10r}: modified reference rating life.
· L_{10r,j}: modified reference rating life of row j, computed from its internal load distribution and life modification factor.
· L_{10r,p}: contribution of raceway p (inner or outer) to a row life.

No quantity is labelled L_{10r} unless the per-row and per-raceway contributions have been computed according to the normative formulae.

2.5.2 Multi-Row Assembly

The full multi-row formula of ISO 16281:2025 (Formula 14) assembles the inner- and outer-raceway contributions of each row inside the summation, with exponents -9/10 and -3/10 respectively, before the outer Weibull combination with e = 10/9:

L_{10r} = \left[ \sum_{j} \left( L_{10r,j,\text{inner}}^{-9/10} + L_{10r,j,\text{outer}}^{-9/10} \right)^{-10/9} \right]^{-9/10}

The precise normalisation, the treatment of the life modification factor a_{\text{ISO}} per row, and the assumptions on the Weibull slopes are taken from the standard. The simplified Weibull sum

L_{10r} = \left( \sum_j L_{10r,j}^{-e} \right)^{-1/e}

is retained only as a conceptual illustration, with the explicit disclaimer that it is not a normative implementation.

2.5.3 Damage Accumulation

The corrected life is not computed independently at each instant of the cycle and then compared as if it represented the vehicle life. A usage history and a damage accumulation rule consistent with the retained hypotheses are defined. The Palmgren–Miner linear rule

D = \sum_k \frac{n_k}{N_k}

is adopted as a cumulative-damage hypothesis; it is not a consequence of ISO 16281:2025. Failure is predicted at D \geq 1. The distinction between the reference life for a single load state, the modified reference life, and the accumulated damage over a spectrum is maintained throughout.

