2.6 Load Distribution Indicators and Falsification Criteria

2.6.1 Primary and Secondary Indicators

The coefficient of variation CV_Q = \sigma_Q/\bar{Q} is insufficient because it can decrease as the number of active contacts increases, even if the maximum load increases. The thesis uses:

Q_{\max} = \max_i Q_i, \qquad \frac{Q_{\max}}{\bar{Q}_{\text{active}}}

H_Q = \sum_i \left( \frac{Q_i}{\sum_j Q_j} \right)^2

and two concentration indices:

C_3 = \sum_i \left( \frac{Q_i}{\sum_j Q_j} \right)^3, \qquad
C_{10/3} = \sum_i \left( \frac{Q_i}{\sum_j Q_j} \right)^{10/3}

The primary indicator for H1 is C_{10/3}, chosen as a conservative majorant of load sensitivity, not as a life predictor. The indicator C_3 is reported alongside because it is linked to the ball-bearing load–life exponent. Neither indicator substitutes for the ISO life calculation. The per-row distribution and the angular distribution are published separately; no global sum masks an overloaded row.

2.6.2 Falsification Scenarios and Decision Thresholds

F1 — Representative load cases. If C_{10/3} is not reduced over the majority of the declared load cases, or if Q_{\max}/\bar{Q}_{\text{active}} increases by more than 10% at any declared case, H1 is rejected.

F2 — High preload. If P_{\text{net}} disappears or becomes negative at maximum preload, H2 is rejected.

F3 — Feasibility. If the optimum of k_f lies at the boundary of the declared domain, the architecture is not necessarily unrealisable: the domain may be too narrow or the optimum constrained. This scenario triggers a domain extension, not an automatic rejection.

F4 — Edge contact. If the Hertz validity criterion is violated for any contact retained in the life calculation, that contact is removed and the corresponding life result is declared invalid. If more than a declared fraction (to be fixed at the start of the campaign) of active contacts are removed, H3 is rejected.

F5 — Contamination and viscosity. If the gain disappears or reverses under degraded lubricant conditions, H5 is rejected.

F6 — Geometric defects. Realistic eccentricity (10–20 µm) and misalignment (1–2 mrad) are tested explicitly.

F7 — Load transients. Numerical convergence and contact validity are checked under rapid load changes. Convergence in a small number of iterations is a necessary but not sufficient condition; dynamic stability is a separate question outside the scope of the quasi-static framework.

F8 — Stiffness degradation. Progressive loss of flexure stiffness is modelled as a parametric perturbation.

F9 — Load-dependent compliance. The behaviour at the 90th and 95th percentile of the load spectrum is examined. The percentile thresholds are parametric scenarios until a measured load history is available; without such a history they do not constitute an empirical claim.

Decision thresholds (exploratory, not universal).

Indicator Exploratory threshold Rejection if
C_{10/3} reduction \geq 10\% vs Comparator A < 3\%
Q_{\max}/\bar{Q}_{\text{active}} No increase Increase > 10\%
Net gain P_{\text{net}} \geq 2\% and > U_{\text{total}} < 0.5\% or loss
L_{10r} (ISO 16281) \geq L_{10r,\text{A}} < L_{10r,\text{A}}
K_{\text{eq}} \geq 95\% \, K_{\text{eq,A}} < 90\% \, K_{\text{eq,A}}
Edge contact No contact removed Any removal from life calculation

The scientific decision rule for energy is \Delta P_{\text{net}} > U_{\text{total}}, where U_{\text{total}} is constructed from a documented uncertainty budget. The 2% threshold is an engineering target, not a universal detectability limit.

