2.8 Verification Protocol

The coupling is introduced only after independent verification of four modules.

M1 — Elliptical contact solver. Function (Q, \rho_{I,x}, \rho_{I,y}, \rho_{II,x}, \rho_{II,y}, E^*) \mapsto (a, b, p_0, \delta). Mandatory tests: circular limit with the correct semi-axis and maximum pressure (0.274 mm and 6.35 GPa for the declared benchmark), known elliptical contact, permutation test that checks the transformation of the ellipse orientation and not only the equality of two scalars, and verification of curvature signs on the actual inner and outer geometries. Pass criterion: relative error < 10^{-4} on the available analytical cases.

M2 — Ring and flexures. Dimensionally consistent derivation of the ring Green's function with explicit load definitions; assembly of the stiffness matrix with flexures; and full-field validation against a three-dimensional finite-element model under sectorial loading. Pass criteria: equivalent stiffness deviation < 10\%, and modal correlation coefficient > 0.95 or normalised L^2 error < 5\% on the radial displacement field. The effective flexure stiffness must be extracted from the finite-element model and compared with the analytical value to within 5%.

M3 — Rigid reference bearing. Double-row equilibrium without DLMR and with rigid outer ring. Pass criteria: global force and moment equilibrium to solver tolerance; convergence under mesh and tolerance refinement; order-of-magnitude consistency of contact loads with the literature; per-row and angular distributions published.

M4 — Life prediction. Full ISO 16281:2025 implementation for multi-row ball bearings, with the normative per-row and per-raceway assembly and the Weibull modulus e = 10/9, after consultation of the full normative text. Pass criterion: reproduction of normative reference cases, with the inputs, outputs, and assumptions of those cases published.

2.8.1 External Benchmark

Prior work on large slewing bearings with flexible rings reports a reduction of the maximum contact force from 92.682 kN to 77.351 kN, approximately 16.5%. This result provides an external order-of-magnitude indication that annular compliance can reduce contact-force concentration. It is not a quantitative target for the DLMR, because the geometry, scale, and loading conditions differ substantially. The DLMR result is compared qualitatively to this indication and quantitatively only to Comparator A.

