2.9 Contributions and Declared Limits

Methodological contribution. A coupled contact–structure–life framework with explicit conventions, a verification protocol for each module, and a falsification campaign with declared thresholds. The framework is reusable for any annular-compliance bearing architecture.

Conditional contribution. If H1–H4 are simultaneously supported after verification, a quantitative assessment of whether outer annular compliance reduces load concentration and net dissipated power without degrading raceway geometry or corrected life, and the identification of the corresponding stiffness range.

Valid negative contribution. If a hypothesis is rejected by the verified framework, the demonstration—by a reproducible and falsifiable model—that the passive DLMR architecture does not outperform the rigid-ring comparator under the declared conditions, and the identification of the physical constraint responsible.

Limits. Prior art exists for load redistribution through annular compliance (EP3163100A1); the thesis does not claim novelty of the general principle. The industrial reference evolves rapidly; thresholds are relative to a dated 2025 profile. Manufacturing defects are outside the base model and are treated as parametric perturbations. Experimental validation is absent at this stage. No life equation is presented as a certified reproduction of the standard unless the full normative text has been consulted and reference cases reproduced.

