Chapter 3 — Computational Implementation and Verification Protocol

3.1 Status of This Chapter

This chapter describes the implementation of the four modules and the verification tests that must be passed before any DLMR conclusion is drawn. It does not present validated numerical outcomes. An earlier draft presented specific reductions in concentration and net power as validated results; that draft contained a numerical error in the circular-contact benchmark, a dimensional inconsistency in the ring derivation, and a non-normative ISO combination. The numerical outcomes of the earlier draft are withdrawn. They are replaced by a statement of what must be computed and how it will be judged.

3.2 Module M1 — Elliptical Contact Solver

The solver computes a, b, p_0, and \delta from the inputs (Q, \rho_{I,x}, \rho_{I,y}, \rho_{II,x}, \rho_{II,y}, E^*). The ellipticity parameter is obtained by Newton–Raphson iteration on the monotonic function F(\rho), with the complete elliptic integrals evaluated by the arithmetic–geometric mean algorithm.

Verification tests. (i) Circular limit: a = b = (3QR/4E^*)^{1/3} and p_0 = 3Q/(2\pi a^2); the declared benchmark gives 0.274 mm and 6.35 GPa, and the solver must reproduce these to 10^{-4} relative error. (ii) Known elliptical contact: a ball in a groove with conformity 0.52, reproduced against the reference values. (iii) Permutation test: the transformation of the ellipse orientation, not only the equality of the semi-axes. (iv) Curvature sign verification: the signs are checked separately on the inner and outer geometries, not assumed.

3.3 Module M2 — Ring and Flexures

Derivation. The ring Green's function is derived from the curved-beam equilibrium equation with an explicit load definition in N/rad or N/m. The 1/\pi prefactor is obtained from the Fourier normalisation of the load, not introduced after the fact. The derivation is documented with units at every step.

Validation. The Green's function is compared to a three-dimensional finite-element model of the ring alone under sectorial loading. The comparison uses the full radial displacement field, not a single scalar. The pass criteria are stated in Section 2.8.

Flexure stiffness. The analytical fixed-guided value k_f = 12EI_f/L_f^3 \approx 1.06 \times 10^5 N/m is compared with the effective stiffness extracted from a three-dimensional finite-element model of the integrated flexure. Agreement to within 5% is required before the analytical value is used in the coupled model.

3.4 Module M3 — Rigid Reference Bearing

The double-row equilibrium is solved without the DLMR and with a rigid outer ring. The output includes the global force and moment equilibrium residuals, the per-row contact loads, and the angular distribution. Convergence is demonstrated under mesh and tolerance refinement. Order-of-magnitude consistency with the literature is checked. No life conclusion is drawn from M3 alone.

3.5 Module M4 — Life Prediction

The full ISO 16281:2025 implementation is checked against normative reference cases after consultation of the full normative text. The inputs, outputs, and assumptions of each reference case are published. The simplified Weibull combination is retained only as an illustration and is not used to produce L_{10r}.

3.6 Module M5 — Coupled DLMR Analysis (Deferred)

The coupled analysis is attempted only after M1–M4 are verified. It uses the fixed-point iteration described in Section 2.3.3 and reports the convergence history, the residuals, and the comparison with a monolithic Newton reference. The output includes the per-row contact loads, the angular distribution, the primary and secondary concentration indicators, the per-channel energy balance, and the ISO life with its per-row and per-raceway decomposition.

3.7 Module M6 — Robustness Campaign (Deferred)

The robustness campaign tests the scenarios F1–F9 on the verified coupled model. The results are reported with the uncertainty budget, not as point estimates.

---

Chapter 4 — Planned Falsification Campaign and Decision Framework

4.1 Structure of the Campaign

The falsification campaign is organised in three stages. Stage 1 verifies M1–M4 independently. Stage 2 applies the verified M1–M4 to the coupled DLMR analysis (M5) and compares the outcome to Comparator A. Stage 3 applies the robustness perturbations (M6) and reports which scenarios are triggered.

4.2 Primary and Secondary Indicators

The primary indicator for H1 is C_{10/3}, with C_3 and Q_{\max}/\bar{Q}_{\text{active}} as secondary diagnostics. The primary indicator for H2 is P_{\text{net}} computed from the complete accounting identity. The primary indicator for H4 is the ISO L_{10r}. The per-row and angular distributions are always published alongside the aggregate indicators.

4.3 Decision Rules

The decision rules of Section 2.6.2 are applied mechanically. A hypothesis is rejected if any rejection threshold is met. A hypothesis is supported if all rejection thresholds are avoided and the declared exploratory thresholds are met. A hypothesis is undetermined if the exploratory thresholds are not met but the rejection thresholds are not triggered; in that case the operating domain is declared insufficient for a conclusion, and the campaign is not extended to claim a benefit.

4.4 Reporting Requirements

Every reported result includes: the input load case; the contact solver version and verification status; the structural model version and validation status; the per-row and angular distributions; the uncertainty budget; and the list of contacts removed from the life calculation for Hertzian validity. Results that cannot be reproduced from these artefacts are not reported as results.

4.5 Status of the Numerical Outcomes

The numerical outcomes of an earlier draft (specific reductions in C_{10/3}, Q_{\max}, P_{\text{net}}, and L_{10r}) are reclassified as illustrative numerical targets — not yet validated. They are not part of the falsification campaign. They are not cited as evidence for or against any hypothesis. They may be reproduced in a separate working document once the modules are verified, at which point they will be reported with the artefacts listed in Section 4.4.

4.6 Anticipated Failure Modes

Three failure modes are anticipated. First, the ring Green's function may fail the full-field validation against finite elements; in that case the modal model is replaced by a shell model, and the coupled analysis is delayed. Second, the ISO multi-row implementation may fail to reproduce a reference case; in that case M4 is corrected before M5. Third, the coupled fixed-point iteration may fail to converge under high load; in that case a monolithic Newton method with line search is used and the fixed-point results are not reported. In all three cases, the failure is documented and treated as part of the scientific contribution.

---

Chapter 5 — Discussion and Conclusions

5.1 Methodological Position

This thesis is a research programme and a verification protocol, not a demonstration of DLMR superiority. The methodological position is that a negative result, obtained from a verified and reproducible framework, is scientifically informative and preferable to a positive result obtained from an unverified one.

5.2 Status of the Hypotheses

At the time of writing, H1–H5 are open. They will be decided by the falsification campaign described in Chapter 4, after M1–M4 are verified. The earlier draft's claim that H1–H4 were supported is withdrawn, because the supporting computations were not reproducible from the document.

5.3 Contributions

Methodological. A coupled framework with explicit conventions, a per-module verification protocol, and a falsification campaign with declared decision rules. The framework is reusable for any annular-compliance bearing architecture.

Conditional. If the campaign supports H1–H4, a quantitative assessment of whether DLMR compliance produces a net benefit under the declared conditions, and the identification of the stiffness range in which it does so.

Valid negative. If the campaign rejects a hypothesis, the identification—by a verified framework—of the physical constraint responsible, which redirects effort toward alternative mechanisms.

5.4 Limitations

The ring Green's function remains a working hypothesis pending independent derivation and finite-element validation. The tangential traction model is simplified, and the rolling, seal, and drag contributions are declared but not yet quantified. The ISO implementation must be checked against the full normative text before it is used in M5. Material fatigue properties are nominal and must be replaced by documented S–N data. Experimental validation is absent. Prior art for annular compliance exists, and the thesis does not claim novelty of the general principle.

5.5 Future Work

Immediate priorities: complete the M1 circular benchmark with the corrected values; derive and validate the ring Green's function with dimensionally consistent load definitions; verify the ISO Formula 14 implementation against reference cases. Only after these are complete should the coupled DLMR analysis and the robustness campaign proceed. Experimental validation on a bench comparison against Comparator A is the natural next step after the numerical campaign.

