B.2 Derivation of the Circular Hertzian Limit

For a sphere of radius R in contact with a plane under load Q, the two principal relative curvatures are equal: \rho_{I,x} = \rho_{I,y} = 1/R, \rho_{II,x} = \rho_{II,y} = 0. Thus \Sigma\rho = 2/R and F(\rho) = 0.

Ellipticity. Substituting F(\rho) = 0 into (B.1) and observing that the numerator vanishes only when (1/\kappa^2+1)E = 2F_e, the unique solution in (0,1] is \kappa = 1. The contact is circular.

Semi-axis. With \kappa = 1: \mathcal{E} = \mathcal{F} = \pi/2, a^* = (2 \cdot \pi/2/(\pi \cdot 1))^{1/3} = 1. Hence:

a = b = \left[\frac{3Q}{2E^* \cdot (2/R)}\right]^{1/3} = \left(\frac{3QR}{4E^*}\right)^{1/3}.
\tag{B.7}

Maximum pressure. For a circular contact of radius a, the pressure distribution is p(r) = p_0\sqrt{1-(r/a)^2}, with Q = \int p\,dA = (2/3)p_0 \pi a^2. Hence:

p_0 = \frac{3Q}{2\pi a^2}.
\tag{B.8}

Numerical benchmark. With Q = 1000 N, R = 6.35 \times 10^{-3} m, E^* = 231 GPa:

a = \left(\frac{3 \cdot 1000 \cdot 6.35\times 10^{-3}}{4 \cdot 231\times 10^9}\right)^{1/3} = \left(2.0617\times 10^{-11}\right)^{1/3} = 2.742 \times 10^{-4}\ \text{m} = 0.274\ \text{mm},

p_0 = \frac{3 \cdot 1000}{2\pi (2.742\times 10^{-4})^2} = \frac{3000}{4.723\times 10^{-7}} = 6.352\times 10^9\ \text{Pa} = 6.35\ \text{GPa}.

These are the correct benchmark values. The values 0.201 mm and 5.89 GPa reported in an earlier draft are withdrawn. The earlier figures are incompatible with the stated inputs; a solver reproducing them does not satisfy the circular limit.

---

B.3 Dimensional Consistency of the Curved-Beam Equation

Starting point. A thin circular ring of radius R_c, flexural rigidity EI, and membrane rigidity EA, subjected to a radial distributed load q_{\text{arc}}(\theta) per unit arc length [N/m].

Curved-beam equation. The standard form (Timoshenko, Theory of Elastic Stability, Ch. 2) for a thin ring free of membrane coupling is:

\frac{EI}{R_c^4}\left(\frac{d^2}{d\theta^2} + 1\right)^2 w(\theta) = q_{\text{arc}}(\theta).
\tag{B.9}

Dimensional check. The operator (d^2/d\theta^2 + 1)^2 is dimensionless because \theta is dimensionless. Therefore:

\left[\frac{EI}{R_c^4} \cdot w\right] = \frac{\text{N·m}^2}{\text{m}^4}\cdot\text{m} = \frac{\text{N}}{\text{m}} = [q_{\text{arc}}]. \quad \checkmark
\tag{B.10}

Conversion to load per unit angle. Define q_{\text{angle}}(\theta) = q_{\text{arc}}(\theta) \cdot R_c [N/rad]. Then:

\left(\frac{d^2}{d\theta^2} + 1\right)^2 w = \frac{R_c^4}{EI}\,q_{\text{arc}} = \frac{R_c^3}{EI}\,q_{\text{angle}}.
\tag{B.11}

Comparison with the earlier draft. The earlier draft wrote the equation as:

\frac{d^2}{d\theta^2}\left(\frac{d^2 w}{d\theta^2} + w\right) + w = \frac{R_c^4}{EI} q(\theta)

with q in [N/rad]. In that form, the right-hand side has units:

\left[\frac{R_c^4 \cdot q_{\text{angle}}}{EI}\right] = \frac{\text{m}^4 \cdot \text{N/rad}}{\text{N·m}^2} = \frac{\text{m}^2}{\text{rad}}.

The left-hand side has units of w, i.e., [m]. The equation was therefore dimensionally inconsistent. The correct form with q in [N/rad] is (B.11).

Remark. Equation (B.9) is the equation used in the literature for ring buckling under uniform pressure. When the ring is supported on elastic foundations (flexures), the left-hand side is augmented by a term k_f w, and the right-hand side retains the same dimensional structure.

---

B.4 Derivation of the Ring Green's Function

Fourier expansion. Let w(\theta) = \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty} w_n e^{in\theta} and q_{\text{angle}}(\theta) = \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty} q_n e^{in\theta}. Substituting into (B.11):

(n^2 - 1)^2 w_n = \frac{R_c^3}{EI} q_n \quad \Rightarrow \quad w_n = \frac{R_c^3}{EI} \frac{q_n}{(n^2-1)^2}, \quad n \geq 2.
\tag{B.12}

The modes n=0 and n=1 are excluded because (n^2-1)^2 = 0 for those modes; they are the rigid-body modes and do not contribute to the elastic response.

Unit point load. Let a point force P = 1 N be applied at \theta = \theta_l. The load per unit angle is:

q_{\text{angle}}(\theta) = \delta(\theta - \theta_l) \quad [\text{N/rad}],

since \int q_{\text{angle}}\,d\theta = 1 N. (The symbol \delta denotes the Dirac delta with units of rad⁻¹.)

The Fourier coefficients are:

q_n = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} \delta(\theta - \theta_l) e^{-in\theta}\,d\theta = \frac{1}{2\pi} e^{-in\theta_l}.
\tag{B.13}

Note the factor 1/(2\pi), not 1. This is the origin of the 1/\pi prefactor, not an ad hoc correction.

Substituting (B.13) into (B.12):

w_n = \frac{R_c^3}{2\pi EI} \frac{e^{-in\theta_l}}{(n^2-1)^2}.
\tag{B.14}

Summing the series. The real-valued displacement at observation angle \theta_k:

w(\theta_k) = \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \left[w_n e^{in\theta_k} + w_n^* e^{-in\theta_k}\right]
= \frac{R_c^3}{2\pi EI} \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{e^{in(\theta_k-\theta_l)} + e^{-in(\theta_k-\theta_l)}}{(n^2-1)^2}.
\tag{B.15}

Since e^{in\Delta\theta} + e^{-in\Delta\theta} = 2\cos(n\Delta\theta):

G_{kl} = \frac{R_c^3}{\pi EI} \sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{\cos n(\theta_k-\theta_l)}{(n^2-1)^2}.
\tag{B.16}

This is the radial Green's function for a unit point load, and the 1/\pi prefactor is now derived from the Fourier normalisation.

Dimensional check. R_c^3/(\pi EI) has units \text{m}^3/(\text{N·m}^2) = \text{m/N}, which is the correct unit for a displacement per unit force. ✓

Membrane term. The n=0 mode of the extensional equation yields the axisymmetric mean response:

w_{\text{mem}} = \frac{R_c}{2\pi EA}.
\tag{B.17}

Dimensional check: \text{m}/(\text{N}) = \text{m/N}. ✓ The membrane term is added to (B.16) when the ring is loaded axisymmetrically.

Limitation. Equation (B.16) is the radial Green's function for a thin ring in pure bending. It neglects:

· Tangential displacements and their coupling with radial displacements;
· Flexural–extensional coupling in modes n \geq 2;
· Shear deformation;
· Ring cross-section warping.

These effects are captured by a shell model. Equation (B.16) is a working hypothesis and is withheld from the coupled implementation until the validation protocol in Appendix D is passed.

