B.6 Proof of Proposition 2: Regularisation by Flexures

Statement. Let \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times m} be the elastic stiffness matrix of the ring, positive semi-definite with null space \mathcal{N} = \mathrm{span}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}_0, \boldsymbol{\phi}_1\}, where \boldsymbol{\phi}_0, \boldsymbol{\phi}_1 are the rigid-body modes (uniform and differential radial translation). Let \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} be a symmetric matrix that is positive definite on \mathcal{N}. Then \mathbf{K}_{\text{global}} = \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} + \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} is positive definite.

Proof. Take any \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^m, \mathbf{v} \neq \mathbf{0}. Write \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} + \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp} with \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} \in \mathcal{N} and \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp} \in \mathcal{N}^\perp.

\mathbf{v}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{global}} \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{v}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} \mathbf{v}.
\tag{B.24}

Since \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} is positive semi-definite with null space \mathcal{N}, the first term equals \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp}, which is non-negative and zero if and only if \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp} = \mathbf{0}.

The second term: write \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} in block form with respect to the decomposition \mathbb{R}^m = \mathcal{N} \oplus \mathcal{N}^\perp:

\mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} = \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B} \\ \mathbf{B}^\top & \mathbf{C} \end{pmatrix},

with \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times 2} positive definite (by hypothesis). For any \mathbf{v} = (\mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}}, \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp}):

\mathbf{v}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{flexures}} \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}}^\top \mathbf{A} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} + 2\mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}}^\top \mathbf{B} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp} + \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp}^\top \mathbf{C} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp}.
\tag{B.25}

This is strictly positive if \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} \neq \mathbf{0} (since \mathbf{A} is positive definite, \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}}^\top \mathbf{A} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} > 0, and the cross term can be bounded below by -\mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}}^\top \mathbf{A} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} by completing the square in \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}}^\perp). If \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}} = \mathbf{0}, then \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp} \neq \mathbf{0}, and the first term \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} \mathbf{v}_{\mathcal{N}^\perp} > 0. In both cases, \mathbf{v}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\text{global}} \mathbf{v} > 0. \square

Scope. This proof establishes algebraic well-posedness of \mathbf{K}_{\text{global}}. It does not establish that:

· \mathbf{K}_{\text{ring}}^{(el)} is correctly derived from the correct Green's function;
· the real flexures produce the assumed positive-definite support;
· the flexure stiffness k_f corresponds to the physical assembly.

These are separate verifications in module M2 (Appendix D).

