B.7 Flexure Stiffness for the Fixed-Guided Boundary Condition

Statement. A prismatic beam of length L_f, flexural rigidity EI_f, with both ends fixed against rotation and one end displaced transversely by \delta under a transverse force F (fixed-guided boundary condition), has:

F = \frac{12 EI_f}{L_f^3}\,\delta.
\tag{B.26}

Derivation. The Euler–Bernoulli equation for the beam is EI_f w'''' = 0 with w(0) = 0, w'(0) = 0, w(L_f) = \delta, w'(L_f) = 0. The general solution is w(x) = c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2 + c_3 x^3. The four boundary conditions give:

c_0 = 0,\quad c_1 = 0,\quad c_2 L_f^2 + c_3 L_f^3 = \delta,\quad 2 c_2 L_f + 3 c_3 L_f^2 = 0.

Solving: c_3 = -2\delta/L_f^3, c_2 = 3\delta/L_f^2. The shear force at the ends is V = -EI_f w''' = -6 EI_f c_3 = 12 EI_f \delta/L_f^3. Hence (B.26). \square

Numerical value. With E = 210 GPa, b_f = 1.2 \times 10^{-3} m, t_f = 0.6 \times 10^{-3} m, L_f = 8 \times 10^{-3} m:

I_f = \frac{b_f t_f^3}{12} = \frac{1.2 \times 10^{-3} \cdot (0.6 \times 10^{-3})^3}{12} = 2.16 \times 10^{-14}\ \text{m}^4,

k_f = \frac{12 \cdot 210\times 10^9 \cdot 2.16\times 10^{-14}}{(8\times 10^{-3})^3} = \frac{5.4432 \times 10^{-2}}{5.12 \times 10^{-7}} \approx 1.063 \times 10^5\ \text{N/m}.

The cantilever formula 3EI_f/L_f^3 would give 2.66 \times 10^4 N/m, a factor of 4 too small. The cantilever is the wrong model for the DLMR flexures, and the earlier draft's value of 1.2 \times 10^5 N/m was not derived from either formula; it is withdrawn.

Caveat. Equation (B.26) assumes: (i) uniform cross-section, (ii) idealised boundary conditions, (iii) linear elasticity, (iv) no interaction with adjacent sectors, (v) bending-dominated response. The effective stiffness of the integrated flexure differs from (B.26) and must be extracted from a 3D finite-element model. Agreement to within 5% between the analytical and effective values is required in module M2.

