B.9 Positive Definiteness of the Sliding Dissipation

Statement. Define the sliding dissipation as:

P_{sl,\text{diss}} = -\sum_c \int_{A_c} \boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} \cdot \mathbf{v}_{sl,c}\,dA,
\tag{B.30}

where \boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} is the tangential traction exerted by body 2 on body 1 (in the contact plane), and \mathbf{v}_{sl,c} = \mathbf{v}_1 - \mathbf{v}_2 is the relative velocity of body 1 with respect to body 2. Then P_{sl,\text{diss}} \geq 0 at every point of the contact, with equality if and only if \mathbf{v}_{sl,c} = \mathbf{0}.

Proof. In a Coulomb-friction contact, the tangential traction is opposite to the relative slip velocity: \boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} = -\mu p_c \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{sl,c} in the sliding region, where \mu is the friction coefficient, p_c is the contact pressure, and \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{sl,c} = \mathbf{v}_{sl,c}/|\mathbf{v}_{sl,c}|. Then:

\boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} \cdot \mathbf{v}_{sl,c} = -\mu p_c |\mathbf{v}_{sl,c}| \leq 0.
\tag{B.31}

Substituting into (B.30): P_{sl,\text{diss}} = \sum_c \int_{A_c} \mu p_c |\mathbf{v}_{sl,c}|\,dA \geq 0. Equality holds if and only if |\mathbf{v}_{sl,c}| = 0 throughout the contact (pure rolling). \square

Scope. This proof assumes the Coulomb friction model. Under more complex traction laws (e.g., EHL with a limiting shear stress), the sign of \boldsymbol{\tau}_c^{(1)} \cdot \mathbf{v}_{sl,c} remains non-positive because friction always opposes relative motion, but the quantitative result depends on the traction model. The simplified traction model used in phase N1 must be declared; validation against a full EHL model is reserved for phase N2.

Incomplete accounting. P_{sl,\text{diss}} is not the total bearing dissipation. The complete accounting identity is:

P_{\text{total}} = P_{rr} + P_{sl,\text{diss}} + P_{\text{seal}} + P_{\text{drag}} + P_{\text{flex}},
\tag{B.32}

with each channel either computed or declared as an estimate with an uncertainty. The net gain is P_{\text{net}} = P_{\text{total,ref}} - P_{\text{total,DLMR}} (both evaluated on the same cycle). A reduction on one channel is not a net gain.

