Appendix C — Contact Solver Verification Test Cases (Module M1)

C.1 Circular Limit

Input. Q = 1000 N, R = 6.35 \times 10^{-3} m, E^* = 231 \times 10^9 Pa.

Expected. a = b = 0.274\ \text{mm}, p_0 = 6.35\ \text{GPa}, \delta = 0.066\ \text{mm} (the normal approach, computed from the Hertz relation \delta = a^2/R for a sphere on a plane).

Pass criterion. Relative error < 10^{-4} on a, b, p_0, and \delta.

Withdrawn values. The earlier draft reported a = 0.201 mm and p_0 = 5.89 GPa for the same inputs. Those values are incorrect and are not accepted as a pass of M1.

C.2 Known Elliptical Contact

Input. Ball in a groove with conformity f_g = R_{g,\text{transverse}}/(2R_b) = 0.52, R_b = 6.35 mm, R_{g,\text{rolling}} = 50 mm, Q = 2000 N, E^* = 231 GPa.

Expected. The ellipticity parameter and semi-axes are taken from the reference solution in Harris & Kotzalas, Rolling Bearing Analysis, 5th ed., Table 6.1 (or equivalent). The exact reference values must be documented in the implementation's test suite before the test is run.

Pass criterion. Relative error < 10^{-4} on a, b, p_0, and \kappa.

C.3 Permutation Test

Input. The same physical contact with the (x,y) axes swapped.

Expected. The ellipse is rotated by 90°, and the numerical values of a, b, and p_0 are unchanged. The orientation of the ellipse must be checked (not only the equality of the two scalars), because an incorrect convention could produce identical scalars with an incorrect orientation.

Pass criterion. Orientation angle error < 10^{-6} rad; scalar values identical to machine precision.

C.4 Curvature Sign Verification

Input. Two geometries: (i) outer raceway with R_{g,\text{rolling}} > 0 and R_{g,\text{transverse}} < 0; (ii) inner raceway with R_{g,\text{rolling}} < 0 and R_{g,\text{transverse}} < 0 (both curvatures negative for the inner groove when viewed from the ball).

Expected. The signed curvature sum \Sigma\rho and curvature difference F(\rho) are computed explicitly for each geometry and verified against the textbook values. The ellipticity parameter \kappa and the semi-axes are distinct for the two geometries.

Pass criterion. Curvature signs match the explicit construction; \kappa and a, b match the reference for each geometry.

C.5 Validity Check on Groove Margins

Input. A contact near the edge of the groove with Q such that a approaches a_{\text{margin}}.

Expected. The solver flags the contact as non-Hertzian when a \geq a_{\text{margin}} or b \geq b_{\text{margin}} and returns a flag, not a result.

Pass criterion. Flag is raised at the correct threshold; no result is returned for a flagged contact.

