Appendix D — Ring and Flexure Validation Protocol (Module M2)

D.1 Derivation Documentation

The ring Green's function derivation is documented with units at every step, following Appendix B.3 and B.4. The derivation file contains:

1. The curved-beam equation in the form (B.11), with a dimensional analysis of every term.
2. The Fourier expansions of w and q_{\text{angle}}, with the units of each coefficient.
3. The Dirac delta load definition (B.13), with explicit units for \delta(\theta - \theta_l).
4. The modal response (B.14), with a dimensional check.
5. The real-valued reconstruction (B.15)–(B.16), with the 1/\pi prefactor traced to the Fourier normalisation.
6. The membrane term (B.17), with its units.
7. The Roark comparison (Appendix B.5), with the factor of 2 between radial deflection and diameter change.

D.2 Finite-Element Reference

A 3D finite-element model of the ring alone is built with:

· Element type: quadratic hexahedral or quadratic tetrahedral (documented choice).
· Mesh: at least four elements through the ring thickness; at least 180 sectors around the circumference (mesh independence verified).
· Boundary conditions: the ring is loaded by a single radial force at one sector, with the opposite sector constrained in the radial direction to remove the rigid-body mode; the other four rigid-body modes are constrained by minimal supports.
· Load: unit radial force P = 1 N applied at one sector.

The radial displacement field u_r(\theta) is extracted at the same sector grid used by the modal model.

D.3 Comparison Criteria

· Equivalent stiffness deviation. The equivalent radial stiffness at the load sector, defined as P/u_r(\theta_{\text{load}}), must agree with the modal prediction to within 10%.
· Modal correlation coefficient. The correlation between the FE and modal radial displacement fields must exceed 0.95.
· Normalised L^2 error. The normalised L^2 error on the radial displacement field, \|u_r^{\text{FE}} - u_r^{\text{modal}}\|/\|u_r^{\text{FE}}\|, must be below 5%.

The second and third criteria are complementary: the correlation coefficient checks the mode shape, while the L^2 error checks the amplitude. A model that "hits the right number" by error compensation would fail the correlation check even if the equivalent stiffness matched.

D.4 Flexure Stiffness Extraction

A 3D finite-element model of a single flexure is built with the actual boundary conditions of the DLMR assembly: the two ends are rigidly connected to the ring and to the crown, and the crown-side connection is displaced transversely by a unit displacement.

The effective stiffness is k_f^{\text{FE}} = F/\delta, where F is the reaction force at the displaced end. The analytical value from Appendix B.7 is k_f^{\text{analytic}} = 1.063 \times 10^5 N/m. Agreement to within 5% is required before the analytical value is used in the coupled model.

D.5 Flexure Fatigue

The flexure stress concentration at the fillets is computed from the 3D model. A documented S–N curve for 100Cr6 at the relevant hardness is required, or a finite-life fatigue method appropriate to the flexure geometry. The generic fatigue limit of 0.9 GPa at 10^7 cycles is not accepted as a design input without a documented source and without accounting for fillet stress concentration, mean stress, residual stress, and surface treatment.

