Appendix E — ISO 16281:2025 Multi-Row Assembly (Module M4)

E.1 Notation

The thesis distinguishes:

· L_{10}: basic rating life (ISO 281).
· L_{10r}: modified reference rating life (ISO 16281:2025).
· L_{10r,j}: modified reference rating life of row j.
· L_{10r,p}: contribution of raceway p (inner or outer) to a row life.

No quantity is labelled L_{10r} unless the per-row and per-raceway contributions have been computed according to the normative formulae.

E.2 Full Multi-Row Assembly

The multi-row assembly follows ISO 16281:2025, Formula 14. For a multi-row ball bearing, the modified reference rating life is:

L_{10r} = \left[ \sum_{j} \left( L_{10r,j,\text{inner}}^{-9/10} + L_{10r,j,\text{outer}}^{-9/10} \right)^{-10/9} \right]^{-9/10},
\tag{E.1}

with the per-row and per-raceway lives computed from the internal load distribution of each row, the life modification factor a_{\text{ISO}} for each row, and the Weibull modulus e = 10/9. The precise normalisation, the treatment of the a_{\text{ISO}} factor, and the load-dependent exponents are taken from the normative text.

An earlier draft used the simplified relation:

L_{10r} = \left( \sum_j L_{10r,j}^{-e} \right)^{-1/e}
\tag{E.2}

This is not the normative multi-row implementation. It is retained only as a conceptual illustration and is explicitly disclaimed in the main text.

E.3 Verification Cases

The implementation is verified by reproducing normative reference cases from the standard. The following reference cases are required:

1. Single-row ball bearing, pure radial load. The computed L_{10r} matches the standard's worked example to within 0.1%.
2. Single-row ball bearing, combined load. Same criterion.
3. Double-row ball bearing, combined load. The computed per-row and per-raceway lives, and the assembled L_{10r}, match the standard's worked example to within 0.1%.

For each case, the inputs (geometry, loads, lubricant, contamination, life modification factor) and the outputs (per-row and per-raceway lives, assembled L_{10r}) are published with the verification result. A verification that reproduces the standard's output without publishing the inputs is not accepted.

E.4 Damage Accumulation

The Palmgren–Miner rule D = \sum_k n_k/N_k is a cumulative-damage hypothesis, not a consequence of ISO 16281:2025. The thesis states this explicitly. The damage calculation is performed on a declared usage spectrum; without such a spectrum, the damage calculation is deferred.

