Appendix F — Energy Balance Accounting

F.1 Complete Accounting Identity

P_{\text{total}} = P_{rr} + P_{sl,\text{diss}} + P_{\text{seal}} + P_{\text{drag}} + P_{\text{flex}},
\tag{F.1}

P_{\text{net}} = P_{\text{total,ref}} - P_{\text{total,DLMR}}.
\tag{F.2}

F.2 Required Reporting for Each Channel

For each dissipative channel, the following are reported:

1. The physical model used (equation or reference).
2. The inputs (loads, speeds, lubricant properties, seal characteristics).
3. The computed value for Comparator A and for the DLMR.
4. The uncertainty of the estimate.
5. Whether the value is computed from a physical model or estimated from a declared correlation.

A channel that is not computed is declared as an estimate and is included in the accounting with its uncertainty. A channel that is omitted is a defect of the accounting, not a null contribution.

F.3 Uncertainty Budget Structure

The combined expanded uncertainty U_{\text{total}} on P_{\text{net}} is constructed from:

· Modelling uncertainty. The difference between the phase-N1 simplified traction model and a phase-N2 EHL model, estimated from the literature or from a sensitivity study.
· Numerical uncertainty. The convergence tolerance of the coupled iteration, propagated to P_{\text{net}}.
· Parametric uncertainty. The uncertainty on the inputs (friction coefficient, lubricant viscosity, contact conformity).
· Estimation uncertainty. The uncertainty on the channels that are estimated rather than computed.

The combination rule (linear or quadrature) is declared. The decision rule is \Delta P_{\text{net}} > U_{\text{total}}; if the estimated gain does not exceed the combined uncertainty, no positive conclusion is drawn.

F.4 Partial-Channel Warning

A reduction on one channel (for example, sliding) is not, by itself, a net gain. The net gain must be computed on the complete accounting identity. In particular, if the flexure hysteresis P_{\text{flex}} is of the same order as the reduction in P_{sl,\text{diss}}, the net gain may be zero or negative. An earlier draft reported a 6.2% reduction in sliding dissipation, a flexure dissipation of 1.8%, and a 4.1% net gain without publishing the complete accounting; that conclusion is withdrawn.

