Appendix G — Falsification Scenario Specification

G.1 General Structure

Each scenario is specified by:

· The hypothesis it tests (H1–H5).
· The perturbation applied.
· The indicator(s) monitored.
· The threshold for rejection.
· The procedure if the threshold is met.

G.2 Scenario Specifications

F1 — Representative combined loads.

· Hypothesis: H1.
· Perturbation: none; the baseline load cases from a declared matrix (radial, axial, moment) are run.
· Primary indicator: C_{10/3}.
· Rejection: C_{10/3} is not reduced over the majority of declared cases, or Q_{\max}/\bar{Q}_{\text{active}} increases by more than 10% at any declared case.
· Procedure if rejected: H1 is rejected; the DLMR is not claimed to equalise loads under the declared load range.

F2 — High preload.

· Hypothesis: H2.
· Perturbation: preload increased to its maximum design value.
· Indicator: P_{\text{net}}.
· Rejection: P_{\text{net}} disappears or becomes negative.
· Procedure if rejected: H2 is rejected in the high-preload range; a narrower claim is retained.

F3 — Feasibility of the optimum.

· Hypothesis: feasibility of the DLMR architecture.
· Perturbation: none; the optimal k_f is located in the declared domain.
· Rejection: automatic rejection is not triggered by an optimum at the boundary. If the optimum lies at the boundary, the declared domain is widened and the campaign is re-run. If the optimum still lies at the boundary of the widened domain, the architecture is declared mechanically constrained, not unrealisable.
· Procedure if constrained: the constraint is documented; a claim of "net benefit is achievable within the declared material and geometry domain" is not made.

F4 — Edge contact.

· Hypothesis: H3.
· Perturbation: none; every active contact is checked against a_{\text{margin}} and b_{\text{margin}}.
· Indicator: number of contacts removed from the life calculation.
· Rejection: any removal from the life calculation triggers a re-analysis of that contact with a local FE model. If more than a declared fraction (fixed at the start of the campaign, default 5%) of active contacts require removal, H3 is rejected.
· Procedure if rejected: the life results are declared invalid; the DLMR is not claimed to preserve Hertzian validity.

F5 — Contamination and viscosity.

· Hypothesis: H5.
· Perturbation: lubricant viscosity reduced by 50%; contamination level increased to the declared degraded level.
· Indicator: P_{\text{net}} and C_{10/3}.
· Rejection: P_{\text{net}} gain disappears or reverses; or C_{10/3} reduction falls below 3%.
· Procedure if rejected: H5 is rejected in the degraded-lubricant range; the claim is restricted to clean, well-lubricated conditions.

F6 — Geometric defects.

· Hypothesis: H5.
· Perturbation: eccentricity in the range 10–20 µm; misalignment in the range 1–2 mrad.
· Indicator: C_{10/3}.
· Rejection: C_{10/3} reduction falls below 3%, or C_{10/3} increases relative to Comparator A.
· Procedure if rejected: H5 is rejected in the defect range; the defect magnitude that inverts the benefit is documented.

F7 — Load transients.

· Hypothesis: numerical robustness, not dynamic stability.
· Perturbation: load ramp from 5 kN to 15 kN over 0.1 s.
· Indicator: convergence of the fixed-point iteration at each time step; contact validity.
· Rejection: iteration fails to converge under plain fixed-point or with relaxation, or contacts become invalid. Dynamic stability is a separate question and is outside the quasi-static framework; a claim of dynamic stability is not made.
· Procedure if rejected: relaxation or a monolithic Newton fallback is used; if convergence still fails, the transient case is declared outside the model's validity domain.

F8 — Stiffness degradation.

· Hypothesis: H5.
· Perturbation: flexure stiffness reduced from the nominal value to the lower bound of the manufacturing tolerance.
· Indicator: C_{10/3} and P_{\text{net}}.
· Rejection: C_{10/3} reduction falls below 3%, or P_{\text{net}} gain falls below U_{\text{total}}.
· Procedure if rejected: H5 is rejected for that stiffness value; the range of acceptable stiffness is documented.

F9 — Load-dependent compliance.

· Hypothesis: H1 in the high-load range.
· Perturbation: load cases at the 90th and 95th percentiles of a declared load spectrum.
· Indicator: C_{10/3} and P_{\text{net}}.
· Rejection: C_{10/3} reduction falls below 3% at either percentile.
· Procedure if rejected: the claim of benefit is restricted to the load range below the 90th percentile. The percentile thresholds are parametric until a measured load spectrum is available; without such a spectrum, they are declared scenarios, not empirical claims.

G.3 Indicator Hierarchy

The primary indicators are:

· C_{10/3} for H1.
· P_{\text{net}} for H2.
· Fraction of active contacts removed for H3.
· L_{10r} for H4.

The secondary indicators are C_3, Q_{\max}/\bar{Q}_{\text{active}}, and the per-row and angular distributions. Secondary indicators are reported but do not by themselves trigger rejection; they serve as diagnostics for the primary indicator's result.

