Appendix H — Numerical Implementation Details

H.1 Ellipticity Solver

· Method: Newton–Raphson on F(\rho) - F_{\text{target}} = 0, with a bracketed line search.
· Initial guess: \kappa_0 = 0.8 (away from the singular endpoint \kappa = 1).
· Bracket: [10^{-3}, 1 - 10^{-3}], updated by the line search.
· Convergence: |F(\rho) - F_{\text{target}}| < 10^{-12} and |\kappa_{\text{new}} - \kappa_{\text{old}}| < 10^{-10}.
· Fallback: If Newton–Raphson stalls (stagnation, cycle, or non-finite derivative), a bisection on the bracket is used. The fallback is logged.
· Elliptic integrals: Arithmetic–geometric mean (AGM) algorithm, relative error < 10^{-10}, verified against the standard values at \kappa = 0, 0.5, 0.9, 0.99, 1.

H.2 Coupled Iteration

· Outer loop: Fixed-point iteration on the ring displacement, as described in Section 2.3.3.
· Inner loop: Contact solver at fixed ring geometry, using a semi-smooth Newton method with an active-set strategy.
· Relaxation: If the fixed-point iteration residual does not decrease monotonically, a relaxation factor \omega \in (0, 1) is applied to the update. The relaxation factor is initialised at 1.0 and reduced by a factor of 2 at each non-decreasing step, down to a minimum of 0.1.
· Comparison: A monolithic Newton method with a globalised line search is implemented as a reference. If the fixed-point and monolithic methods produce different results, the difference is reported as an uncertainty contribution.
· Convergence history: The residual at each iteration is logged and published with the results.

H.3 Mesh and Discretisation

· Contact solver: The contact ellipse is discretised at N_a \times N_b integration points for the sliding power computation; the choice of N_a and N_b is verified by convergence study.
· Ring model: The sector grid has N_s sectors; N_s is chosen so that N_s \geq 4 \times n_{\max}, where n_{\max} is the maximum elastic mode retained. The choice of n_{\max} is verified by convergence study on the Green's function.
· Life calculation: The per-row and per-raceway lives are computed from the discretised contact loads; the discretisation error is included in the uncertainty budget.

H.4 Version Control and Reproducibility

Each reported result is accompanied by:

· The version of the contact solver (commit hash or release tag).
· The version of the structural model.
· The version of the ISO 16281 implementation.
· The input files (load cases, geometries, material properties).
· The output files (contact loads, displacements, energies, lives).
· The convergence logs.

A result that cannot be reproduced from these artefacts is not reported as a result.

